ls  guilty still and  he knows he probably could have still  done this better it's not totally  justifiable even though he was having a  panic attack  even though he was having an asthma  attack there were still moments he could  have redeemed himself and he left the  scene  and left someone to die as he says but  the dad doesn't want to hear that the  dad wants to  justify this in his head to protect his  son even going to the extremes comparing  it to when his mother died and that  that put him in shock so i was like yeah  this is part of who you are so clearly  they're going to use that as the case to  defend him but they both get to their  senses here and the right move is to go  to the police station  and it's important to remember michael  put in this call to the police station  first that they were coming so that's  going to be held against him as well but  we get this big twist  that the father of the boy on the  motorcycle is  this crime boss jimmy baxter played by  michael  stuhlbarg so this stops really all  of michael's plan here he knows this is  worse than he could have imagined  because  it's not now just going to be a case of  making sure his son doesn't go to prison  and they can get out of this  and he can use his connects now it's him  versus basically this mob and that  he knows if they ever find out his son  did this he's gonna get  whacked and that's why he panics and  he's like no we gotta go and he's like i  can keep you safe if no one hears about  this so you see how quickly  his character is turning now at all  costs to defend his son but man  you just see that dog just grab the  bloody rag here when michael's cleaning  up the clothes  and the car brutal that's gonna haunt  them and like how the episode ends  it's gonna haunt them about the inhaler  that adam stupidly left  on the street so what's interesting  about this show  i didn't think it was the most thrilling  premiere i don't think it was the most  hooking either but i think  it wa